# Coding practices

Before generating or modifying matched application, test, or implementation-configuration files,
follow `.claude/skills/coding-practices/SKILL.md`.

Detect the affected technology and service, then read:
- the nearest `CLAUDE.md`;
- applicable shared ESLint, Prettier, TypeScript, .NET, and test configuration;
- adjacent production and test code;
- relevant architecture and ADR boundaries.

The trigger should automatically load the skill for matching files when supported by the active
tool. Deterministic compliance remains the responsibility of compiler, analyzers, lint, tests,
and CI.

Preserve existing service ownership, public contracts, strict typing, async/resource safety,
server-side authorization, safe errors, migration safety, and truthful unit/integration/E2E claims.

Prefer existing utilities, ports, adapters, validators, fixtures, and test infrastructure. Keep
controllers/components thin and business logic in the established service/domain layers. Do not
invent abstractions, libraries, infrastructure, or configuration keys without demonstrated need.

Apply the technology-specific C#/.NET, NestJS/TypeScript/Prisma, React, and test guidance in the
canonical skill. Use deterministic tooling for formatting, compilation, linting, analyzers,
database checks, and CI rather than relying on this rule.

Do not activate this rule for documentation-only work, BMAD planning artifacts, Git operations,
status synchronization, PR-description-only work, or skill/rule maintenance unless explicitly
requested.
